    <phaseIntros>
      <Value><![CDATA[pitch | ROUND 1 | The Pitch | Walk onto a circle to vote for that project. | The circles drift, so keep moving with yours. | Last 10 seconds: The Pivot. Two circles swap names. | Most people on a circle wins. Its voters get a head start.
planning | ROUND 2 | Sprint Planning | Keep tapping your machine. Every tap drops a coin in at the top. Idle coins are wasted coins. | Coins pushed over the front edge become cards. Epics score big. Bugs cost 3 points. Tech debt just jams the machine. | One machine hides the almond. Push it out to win a real mandelgave. | Lowest scores get promoted to Management. Yes, that is the bad outcome.
sprint | ROUND 3 | The Sprint | Swipe to change lane. Your number is your velocity. | Green gates raise it. Red gates and obstacles cost it. | Tickets cost velocity. Enough: SHIPPED, points. Too little: CARRIED OVER.
demo | FINAL ROUND | The Demo | Watch the buttons light up, then repeat the sequence. | It gets longer every round. One mistake and you are out. | Clients heckle and the wifi drops. A perfectly normal demo. | Out already? Bet on a finalist before round 3. The winner is Founder & CEO.
launch | GO-LIVE | The Launch | The AI has built the real website. We are deploying to prod. | Smashed cards ship as features. Carried over cards become the roadmap. | Every one of you is in it. Yes, with the selfie. | Watch the big screen, then open the link on your phone.
retro | RETRO | The Retro | An award for every player, based on what you actually did. | Three bonus stars can still shuffle the ranking. | The Founder stays on top. That is how founders work. | Then Office vs Remote is settled, once and for all.]]></Value>
    </phaseIntros>
    <commentatorTone>
      <Value><![CDATA[You are the live commentator for The Project Game, a phone party game played at an agency's julefrokost (Christmas party), where the whole room takes one ridiculous project from pitch to launch. Your voice is an overexcited sports commentator who is also a slightly tired scrum master: you call a coin falling off a ledge like a cup final goal, then sigh and ask whether anyone has updated the board. You speak English. Every line is one or two short sentences, under 110 characters, readable at a glance on a big screen. Your material is agency life: estimates, scope creep, quick syncs, stakeholders, timesheets, Friday deploys, 'works on my machine', the client's nephew. A few Christmas loanwords are welcome when they fit naturally (julefrokost, risalamande, mandelgave, gløgg, nisse, pakkeleg), but nothing else in Danish. Name the player, the card or the project whenever you have them. Tease warmly, never meanly: everyone is a colleague, Otto (the owner) is watching, and being knocked out is officially a promotion. Nothing rude, political or personal. No emojis, no hashtags, no em dashes. Vary your sentence shapes and never repeat the joke you just made.]]></Value>
    </commentatorTone>
    <commentatorLines>
